epr-meta-version: 1
id: app-workspace-runtime-vendor-fence
purpose: >
  The ONE place in the client tree that may know a development workspace
  vendor's hostname convention. Eclipse Che is the runtime we develop inside
  today; it is tooling we happen to use, not part of the elohim protocol, and
  the protocol core must never learn its name. This directory turns a vendor
  convention into ordinary configuration (`doorwayOrigin` / `doorwayUrl`) that
  `@elohim/service` consumes without knowing where it came from. Swapping the
  workspace runtime — for the elohim-native peer runtime this project intends to
  replace it with — should be an edit to `workspace-doorway.ts` and nothing else.
rules:
  - id: vendor-fence-stays-here
    class: inject
    when:
      write: "*.ts"
      contains-any: ["devspaces", "code.ethosengine.com", "-angular-dev",
                     "-hc-dev", "-hc-storage", "Eclipse Che", "isEclipseChe"]
    dedupe-of: app/workspace-runtime/.epr-meta (body — "The boundary" table)
    why: >
      You are editing the vendor fence itself, which is the right place for these
      strings — this is a reminder of the contract, not a complaint. Keep the
      vendor facts as DATA (the `WORKSPACE_HOSTS` list and the `ENDPOINTS` map),
      never as branching logic, and never export a symbol whose NAME carries the
      vendor ("che", "eclipse"). Consumers must only ever see
      `isWorkspaceRuntime()` / `workspaceEndpointOrigin()` /
      `workspaceDoorwayUrl()`. If you are adding a second workspace runtime, add
      entries to the two data tables; if you are adding a NEW endpoint, add it to
      `ENDPOINTS` rather than writing another `.replace()` at a call site — four
      hand-rolled copies of that replace is the drift this directory was created
      to end (2026-08-27).
cites:
  - doorway-auth-posture-declared-stage
