# app/workspace-runtime — the workspace-vendor fence

The client is served from one origin and the doorway from another whenever we
develop inside a workspace runtime that publishes each declared service port at
its own hostname. Only the browser can work out that sibling origin, and only
from a vendor-specific naming convention. This directory owns that convention.

## The boundary

| Layer | May know | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `app/workspace-runtime/` (here) | the vendor's hostname convention | `-angular-dev.` → `-hc-dev.` |
| `@elohim/service`, `elohim-elements`, `doorway-service` | **nothing** — they take an origin as config | `config.doorwayOrigin` |

The protocol core resolving its own origin by sniffing hostnames is the drift
this fence exists to prevent. It had already happened four times in parallel —
`doorway-connection-strategy.ts`, `angular-provider.ts`,
`holochain-client.service.ts` and `password-auth.provider.ts` each carried its
own copy of the same detector and the same `.replace()`, so the convention could
not be changed in one place and two of the copies had already drifted apart in
their SSR guards.

## Not a "proxy"

`useLocalProxy` means the opposite topology — same-origin, reverse-proxied by the
dev server, which is how `/db/*`, `/blob/*` and `/apps/*` are reached. This
module resolves a *different* origin, because a WebSocket upgrade does not
survive that reverse proxy. Both topologies are live simultaneously in a
workspace; calling both "the proxy" is how they get confused.

## Known remaining violation

`app/elohim-library/projects/elohim-service/src/client/angular-provider.ts`
still carries a vendor branch inside `detectClientMode()`. It is reached from
BOTH `app/elohim-app` and `app/lamad`, so removing it means both callers must
pass a resolved `doorwayUrl` from here. Tracked so it is not mistaken for a
place the convention is allowed to live.
